> | Person | What to know / likely angle for you |
> |---|---|
> | **Jeffrey Sun** | Visit organizer, likely your logistical anchor. Use him for timing/room questions and to understand department culture. If he is a grad student or staff organizer, be especially gracious—he will shape the visit’s smoothness. |
> | **Jacquelyn Pless** | Rotman School faculty working at the intersection of **energy, innovation, entrepreneurship, and environmental policy**. Excellent person for discussing clean-tech innovation, firm responses to climate policy, and how management/strategy perspectives differ from econ-department framing. |
> | **Christian Spielmann** | U of T economics-affiliated; likely a junior faculty member or researcher. I’d ask about his current research agenda and how the department is building strength in applied micro / environmental / urban / development areas. |
> | **Karen Clay** | Major environmental/economic historian, associated with **Carnegie Mellon** and NBER circles. Known for work on **pollution, energy, historical development, regulation, and health/environment links**. Very relevant to climate impacts, empirical identification, and long-run adaptation questions. |
> | **Larasati Wulandari** | Lunch organizer and afternoon meeting. Likely a U of T economist/doctoral researcher with interests near applied micro, development, environment, or political economy. Ask what seminars/working groups are most active and what datasets or collaborations people use. |
> | **Benjamin Lauzon** | Lunch participant, likely graduate student or junior researcher. Good source for candid department climate, student interaction, and how visitors’ seminars land with the applied micro/environment group. |
> | **Ayaka Behro** | Likely junior U of T researcher/graduate student. Treat as a potential close reader of your methods. Ask what parts of your warming/GDP meta-analysis resonate with current student research. |
> | **Eduardo Souza-Rodrigues** | Key meeting. U of T economist working in **environmental/resource economics**, often with structural/dynamic modeling and land-use/deforestation-type questions. Strong overlap with climate damages, adaptation, policy counterfactuals, and global environmental change. |
> | **Hugo Cordeau** | Likely junior researcher/graduate student. Good opportunity to discuss technical details: heterogeneity, meta-analysis specification, spatial aggregation, uncertainty, and identification. |
> | **Christoph Semken** | U of T economics faculty; likely interests in macro/spatial/environmental or quantitative applied work. Since he joins dinner, prepare a broader conversation on climate impacts, growth, migration, and how economists should model a warming world. |
